    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1041">

                <pb facs="1288" n="1270" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg1270"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">833.248/1–1746</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sparks</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-17T19:00:00-05:00">January 17, 1946—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>20. In conversation January 8, Assistant Secretary Braden<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d1041-f1"/> agreed with General Arnold<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d1041-f2"/> to approve interim allocation of aircraft to armed
                    forces of Uruguay as follows: 8 B–25’s, 15 P–47’s, 4 C–47’s, 3 C–45’s, 5
                    AT–11’s. Because certain of these types of planes are now in short supply, it
                    may be necessary to substitute other types for them.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Please telegraph Dept whether you approve allocations mentioned above. Subject
                    has been discussed in Dept with Amb Dawson,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d1041-f3"/> who
                    concurs in allocations, subject to Emb approval. Although you will no doubt wish
                    to consult U.S. military air officers, your decision should, of course, take
                    political and economic factors into account.</p>
                <p>The foregoing information is not to be disclosed to Uruguayan officials. For your
                    information questions of procedure on disposal of planes are still to be worked
                    out. Dept will inform you as soon as ground and air force equipment can be made
                    available.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1041-f1" n="2" xml:id="d1041-f1-content">
                <p>Spruille Braden, Assistant Secretary of State for American Republic Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1041-f2" n="3" xml:id="d1041-f2-content">
                <p>Gen. Henry H. Arnold, Commanding General, Army Air Forces.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1041-f3" n="5" xml:id="d1041-f3-content">
                <p>Ambassador Dawson departed for the United States on January 12 and resumed charge
                    in Montevideo on March 25, 1946.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
